{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Review Changed Code",
  "description": "Reviews web and Java changes since origin/main for correctness, maintainability, architecture, DRY opportunities, and project standards.",
  "version": "2",
  "when": {
    "type": "userTriggered"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "From the workspace root, first run `git diff --name-only origin/main -- \"*.ts\" \"*.html\" \"*.scss\" \"*.css\" \"*.java\"` to identify relevant files, then run `git diff --find-renames origin/main -- <changed files>` and read enough surrounding code to understand each change in context. Review only issues introduced or materially worsened by this diff; do not report pre-existing issues unless the change directly interacts with them.\n\nPerform a thorough review for:\n1. Correctness: logic errors, unhandled edge cases, invalid assumptions, data validation, null/undefined handling, resource cleanup, error propagation, concurrency/thread safety, and security-sensitive mistakes.\n2. Maintainability: clear names and intent, focused methods/components, manageable complexity, sensible control flow, testability, useful comments only where intent is non-obvious, and avoidance of brittle coupling or hidden side effects.\n3. Architecture and boundaries: appropriate placement of responsibilities; separation of UI, domain, persistence, and infrastructure concerns; stable, minimal public APIs; correct dependency direction; and reuse of established project abstractions rather than adding parallel patterns. Flag unnecessary framework leakage or cross-layer dependencies.\n4. DRY: duplicated business rules, validation, transformations, error handling, UI markup, configuration, or literals that could diverge. Recommend consolidation only when the duplication represents the same stable concept; do not force abstractions for small, incidental, or one-off similarities.\n5. Performance: unnecessary repeated work, avoidable I/O or network calls, inefficient collections/rendering, memory/resource issues, and inappropriate caching.\n6. Project standards: for web, Angular signals, OnPush, proper typing, tooltip conventions, barrel imports, Tailwind class order, and accessibility; for Java, exception handling, thread safety, naming, and existing project conventions.\n7. Hygiene: dead code, unused imports, stale comments, incomplete tests where behavior changed, and missing observability where failures would otherwise be difficult to diagnose.\n\nPrioritize actionable findings. Group findings by file and give each an error, warning, or suggestion severity, an exact line reference, a concise explanation of impact, and a concrete remediation. Do not invent findings. If there are no actionable findings, say so and briefly note any remaining test or review risk."
  }
}
